 # Masked-Rasterization
-
-
 ## Overview
-Masked-Rasterization is a technique that allows probabilistically existing Gaussians to receive gradients during the blending process, even when they are masked out of the final image. It solves the problem of "stale" Gaussians in pruning pipelines by ensuring that unsampled entities can still update their existence probability based on their virtual contribution, rather than being permanently discarded due to zero alpha filtering. It differs from sibling methods like [Optimal Gaussian Splatting](3293-optimal-gaussian-splatting.md) by applying masks during the blending stage rather than modifying Gaussian attributes (opacity/scale) prior to rasterization, which prevents the loss of gradient flow.
+Masked-Rasterization is a technique that allows probabilistically existing Gaussians to receive gradients during the blending process, even when they are masked out of the final image [S1]. It solves the problem of "stale" Gaussians in pruning pipelines by ensuring that unsampled entities can still update their existence probability based on their virtual contribution, rather than being permanently discarded due to zero alpha filtering [S1]. It differs from sibling methods like [Optimal Gaussian Splatting](3293-optimal-gaussian-splatting.md) by applying masks during the blending stage rather than modifying Gaussian attributes (opacity/scale) prior to rasterization, which prevents the loss of gradient flow [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- A set of 3D Gaussians $G_i$, each defined by center, scale, rotation, and an existence probability $p_i$.
-- A differentiable mask $m_i \in [0, 1]$ sampled for each Gaussian based on $p_i$.
-- Camera parameters for projection.
