 4. **Gradient Propagation:**
-   - For sampled Gaussians ($m_i \approx 1$): Gradients flow to standard attributes ($\mathbf{t}, \mathbf{s}, \mathbf{q}, \alpha, \mathbf{c}$) and the mask.
-   - For masked Gaussians ($m_i \approx 0$): Gradients do **not** flow to standard attributes (position, scale, rotation, opacity) because the effective alpha contribution is zero. However, gradients **do** flow to the mask $m_i$. This allows the existence probability $p_i$ to be updated based on the error signal, even if the Gaussian did not visually contribute to the pixel.
