+- **Mask Sampling:** Masks are sampled from a Bernoulli distribution defined by the existence probability $p_i$. In practice, this is often implemented using the Gumbel-Max trick or straight-through estimator for differentiability [G].
+- **Typical Values:** Pruning ratios of 62.4% (Mip-NeRF360), 67.7% (Tanks & Temples), and 75.3% (Deep Blending) are reported with speedups of 2.05×, 2.19×, and 3.16× respectively, with only a 0.02 PSNR decline on the first two datasets [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Zero Alpha Filtering:** If a Gaussian has zero alpha (due to zero opacity or scale), it is filtered out *before* masked-rasterization. It receives no gradients at all, hindering the update of its existence probability. This is a critical pitfall of previous methods that multiply masks with attributes. Masked-Rasterization avoids this by applying the mask *during* blending, allowing non-zero alpha Gaussians to participate even if masked.
-- **Gradient Isolation:** Masked Gaussians must not update their geometric attributes (position, scale, rotation) to avoid instability. The gradient path to these attributes must be blocked for masked splats.
